WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.110
Film Courage: What did you learn from the writing
process for USE ME movie? Julian Shaw, Actor/Writer/Director: Why I think

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.770
the first treatment wasn't that great
and it was because there was a

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:14.040
fundamental problem which was we were
trying to send the wrong character on an

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.289
arc right I was trying to send the
Ceara character on a certain arc and it

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:23.970
didn't ring true to reality so I don't
want to give spoilers away for when

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.029
people see the film but sometimes when
you're hitting your head against a brick

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:32.130
wall when you're writing it's actually
this fundamental problem of having the

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:38.670
wrong protagonist right the person who
you're making change and transform its

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:43.290
feeling artificial because that's just
it's just not right for the story so

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.469
that really changed from the first draft
of the script where it was very much

00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:51.329
about Ceara changing and it just didn't
feel authentic then I sat down with

00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:55.230
Ceara and I said all right listen who do
you want to be in this movie do you want

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.390
to be Bruce Wayne or do you want to be
the Joker

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:01.140
she said what what are we talking about
I said well I'm trying to make this

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.019
story where you have this big epiphany
and you change and stuff like that and

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:09.240
it kind of feels phony maybe your
character doesn't necessarily change

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.930
fundamentally maybe how we perceive your
character completely changes but maybe

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:18.330
you're just you kind of like the Joker
right this anarchist who is kind of the

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.860
same from beginning to end and she was
like okay I want to be the Joker and I

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.200
was like I bet you do so once we kind of
shifted gears and actually made my

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.829
character more the protagonist meaning
you know the conduit through which the

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:37.229
audience experiences this world
everything changed and the scripts got

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:41.340
much better the whole thing made more
sense and it all felt more authentic so

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.579
yeah the first draft just had a
fundamental problem that was like the

00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:49.860
wrong protagonist and is that are those
notes to that your producer had given

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:53.280
you or you came up with that on your own
for sure no I mean it sort of became

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.540
apparent to all of us ciara Jonathan
green the producer and myself we just

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.040
felt this direction isn't working and it
was it was it was obvious you know that

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.140
something wasn't right so we were very
frank about that yeah and then we kind

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.050
of went in a new direction and it felt a
lot more authentic

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.860
but I wouldn't have got there if I
hadn't written out a conventional

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:21.980
screenplay so even though the film ended
up going in unexpected directions it was

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.900
that foundation of the screenplay that
allowed us to move and actually be

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:33.170
nimble like for instance we did a real
Kickstarter campaign okay

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:38.000
and someone reached out and said I'd
give you a lot of money if I could see a

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.510
video of you and Julie and making out
they reached out to Ceara they were

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:46.030
like if I could see a video of you guys
making outs and teasing me I'll give you

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:52.310
$7,500 Wow okay so that inspired a whole
new storyline which you'll see when you

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:58.460
watch the film but uh yeah you know we
were we were able to to be pretty

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:06.620
flexible with the story also because I
was the lead in it so I had access to

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.380
myself all the time
I mean I was still rewriting it in the

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.160
edit suite there were video diaries in
the film that I actually shot in the

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:17.240
edit suite so the methodology we
employed was really only possible

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.580
because Ceara and I were the leads and
we had access to ourselves all the time

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:26.000
so that's really interesting so if you
see that you're writing a character that

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.350
you really can't implement any kind of
arc maybe that's not the protagonist

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:36.200
maybe they're not meant to change okay
very interesting because you always do

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.670
want a character to go on some kind of
transformation a lock that's why we love

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:45.350
storytelling that's the lessons that we
get from movies and books but maybe

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:49.010
you've picked the wrong character maybe
that character isn't meant to change and

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.580
maybe that's okay that that's a piece of
advice I wish I could have given myself

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:57.530
at the start of the writing process but
you knew that the character of Julianne

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:01.220
could change well it opened up a whole
bunch of new possibilities for the

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:06.260
journey my character could go on just by
shifting the focus a little bit yeah

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:12.230
yeah how long was the screenwriting
process I mean you know honestly

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:18.470
probably six to twelve months of writing
and really exploring the material yeah I

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:22.610
mean it's funny you know the film seems
to just kind of like naturally unfold

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:28.100
like it's it all just really happened
but um right from the first scene it's

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:32.690
actually pretty meticulous how it's all
set up and I hope that's invisible I

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:36.020
hope the audience just feels like it's
spontaneous and it's kind of like this

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:42.500
documentary that kind of got out of
control but no we really we plotted it

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:46.460
pretty carefully and it's funny I've
never felt so in control of a process

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:50.150
creatively even though it feels like
total chaos while you're watching it it

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:53.780
was actually the most I've ever felt
like I really had a strong grip on it

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:59.620
so yeah it's it's sort of an illusion
the chaos of the movie a very calculated

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:05.060
illusion so do you want to be Bruce
Wayne do you want to be the joke it's a

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:09.320
good question it depends on the movie I
may be more the persuading in this movie

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.750
Ceara Lynch is a good Joker I'll let her
be the Joker

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.430
you

